Chapter: Minimax
  cites Games chapter Rng
  cites Games chapter TicTacToe
  cites Games chapter Connect4

 Minimax search engines. Scores are integers: positive favors player 1,
 negative favors player 2. Two engines live here. TicTacToe gets a
 full-depth search: the game is at most nine plies deep, so the recursion
 is bounded by the board itself - the score walk alternates between
 ttt-mm-score and ttt-mm-fold, two frames per ply, never more than about
 twenty frames, safe on the bare-metal stack. Connect4 keeps the iterative
 two-ply engine: a flat loop that tries every legal move, then every legal
 counter-move, and evaluates statically, so call depth never exceeds two
 function calls regardless of game length.

 We say:

Section: Types

  MmConfig = record {
    max-depth  : Integer,
    eval-fn-id : Integer
  }

Section: TicTacToe Full-Depth Search

 Count empty squares remaining on the board.

  ttt-empty-count : TttBoard, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ttt-empty-count (b) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 9 then acc
    else if ttt-empty b i then ttt-empty-count b (i + 1) (acc + 1)
    else ttt-empty-count b (i + 1) acc

 Score a position by searching every line of play to the end of the game.
 Scores are taken from player 1's perspective and discounted by depth so
 the search prefers the fastest win and the slowest loss: a win at depth d
 scores 100 - d, a loss scores d - 100, a draw scores 0.

  ttt-mm-score : TttBoard, Integer -> Integer
  ttt-mm-score (b) (depth) =
    if b.game-over then
      if b.winner == 1 then 100 - depth
      else if b.winner == 2 then depth - 100
      else 0
    else if b.current-player == 1 then ttt-mm-fold b 0 (-1000) depth
    else ttt-mm-fold b 0 1000 depth

 Fold over the nine squares: apply each legal move, score the child
 position one ply deeper, and keep the maximum for player 1 or the
 minimum for player 2.

  ttt-mm-fold : TttBoard, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ttt-mm-fold (b) (col) (acc) (depth) =
    if col >= 9 then acc
    else if ttt-empty b col == False then ttt-mm-fold b (col + 1) acc depth
    else let s = ttt-mm-score (ttt-move b col) (depth + 1)
    in let keep = ttt-mm-keep (b.current-player) s acc
    in ttt-mm-fold b (col + 1) keep depth

  ttt-mm-keep : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ttt-mm-keep (p) (s) (acc) =
    if p == 1 then if s > acc then s else acc
    else if s < acc then s else acc

 Scan all nine squares and return the index of the best-scoring legal
 move for the board's current player.

  ttt-mm-pick : TttBoard, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ttt-mm-pick (b) (col) (best-col) (best-score) =
    if col >= 9 then best-col
    else if ttt-empty b col == False then ttt-mm-pick b (col + 1) best-col best-score
    else let s = ttt-mm-score (ttt-move b col) 1
    in let better = if b.current-player == 1 then s > best-score else s < best-score
    in if better then ttt-mm-pick b (col + 1) col s
    else ttt-mm-pick b (col + 1) best-col best-score

 Public entry point: return the best move index (0-8) for the board's
 current player, searching the complete game tree. The one book move is
 the empty board, where the search would visit nearly a million positions
 to conclude what is already known: the center is a perfect opening.

  ttt-perfect-ai : TttBoard -> Integer
  ttt-perfect-ai (b) =
    if b.game-over then -1
    else if ttt-empty-count b 0 0 == 9 then 4
    else let init = if b.current-player == 1 then -1000 else 1000
    in ttt-mm-pick b 0 (-1) init

Section: Zero-Player Game

 Both seats play the full-depth search. Perfect play from both sides
 always ends in a draw - the only winning move is not to play.

  run-ttt-perfect : TttResult
  run-ttt-perfect =
    ttt-perfect-loop ttt-new 0

  ttt-perfect-loop : TttBoard, Integer -> TttResult
  ttt-perfect-loop (b) (moves) =
    if b.game-over then TttResult { winner = b.winner, moves-made = moves }
    else if moves > 9 then TttResult { winner = b.winner, moves-made = moves }
    else let pos = ttt-perfect-ai b
    in if pos < 0 then TttResult { winner = b.winner, moves-made = moves }
    else ttt-perfect-loop (ttt-move b pos) (moves + 1)

  format-ttt-perfect : TttResult -> Text
  format-ttt-perfect (r) =
    let w = if r.winner == 1 then "X wins"
            else if r.winner == 2 then "O wins"
            else "Draw"
    in "TicTacToe (zero players, perfect play): " & w & " in " & show (r.moves-made) & " moves"

Section: Connect4 Iterative AI

 Static heuristic for Connect4 from player 1's perspective.
 Win = ±1000. Otherwise use center-column preference as tiebreaker.

  c4-static-score : Connect4Board -> Integer
  c4-static-score (b) =
    if b.game-over then
      if b.winner == 1 then 1000
      else if b.winner == 2 then -1000
      else 0
    else c4-positional-score b

 Sum a positional bonus across all filled cells. Center columns score
 higher for player 1, lower for player 2.

  c4-positional-score : Connect4Board -> Integer
  c4-positional-score (b) =
    c4-pos-loop (b.squares) 0 0

  c4-pos-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c4-pos-loop (sq) (idx) (acc) =
    if idx >= 42 then acc
    else let col = idx - (idx / 7) * 7
    in let bonus = c4-col-bonus col
    in let piece = list-at sq idx
    in let delta = if piece == 1 then bonus
                   else if piece == 2 then -bonus
                   else 0
    in c4-pos-loop sq (idx + 1) (acc + delta)

  c4-col-bonus : Integer -> Integer
  c4-col-bonus (col) =
    if col == 3 then 4
    else if col == 2 then 2
    else if col == 4 then 2
    else if col == 1 then 1
    else if col == 5 then 1
    else 0

 Depth-1 minimizing response: opponent picks worst-for-us reply.

  c4-min-response : Connect4Board, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c4-min-response (b) (col) (worst) =
    if col >= 7 then worst
    else if b.game-over then worst
    else if c4-col-full b col then c4-min-response b (col + 1) worst
    else let after = connect4-drop b col
    in let s = c4-static-score after
    in let next-worst = if s < worst then s else worst
    in c4-min-response b (col + 1) next-worst

 Depth-1 maximizing response: player 1 picks best reply.

  c4-max-response : Connect4Board, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c4-max-response (b) (col) (best) =
    if col >= 7 then best
    else if b.game-over then best
    else if c4-col-full b col then c4-max-response b (col + 1) best
    else let after = connect4-drop b col
    in let s = c4-static-score after
    in let next-best = if s > best then s else best
    in c4-max-response b (col + 1) next-best

 Score a single candidate column drop. Two-ply: apply move, then let
 opponent pick their best one-ply reply.

  c4-score-move : Connect4Board, Integer -> Integer
  c4-score-move (b) (col) =
    let after = connect4-drop b col
    in if after.game-over then c4-static-score after
    else if b.current-player == 1 then c4-min-response after 0 1001
    else c4-max-response after 0 (-1001)

 Scan all seven columns, return the best column for current player.

  c4-best-move : Connect4Board, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  c4-best-move (b) (col) (best-col) (best-score) =
    if col >= 7 then if best-col >= 0 then best-col else 3
    else if b.game-over then if best-col >= 0 then best-col else 3
    else if c4-col-full b col then c4-best-move b (col + 1) best-col best-score
    else let s = c4-score-move b col
    in if b.current-player == 1 then
      if s > best-score then c4-best-move b (col + 1) col s
      else c4-best-move b (col + 1) best-col best-score
    else
      if s < best-score then c4-best-move b (col + 1) col s
      else c4-best-move b (col + 1) best-col best-score

 Public entry point: return the best column (0-6) for the current player
 using two-ply iterative search.

  c4-iterative-ai : Connect4Board -> Integer
  c4-iterative-ai (b) =
    if b.game-over then -1
    else let init-score = if b.current-player == 1 then -1002 else 1002
    in c4-best-move b 0 (-1) init-score

